.TH V32C++\-ARRAY 3v32 "October 16, 2026" "v32c++ 20261016-dev" "v32c++ Library"
.SH NAME
v32c++\-array \- std::array in v32c++, a fixed-size container
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <array>
.PP
.BI "std::array< " T ", " N " > " name ;
.BI "std::array< " T ", " N " > " name " = { " "value, ..." " };"
.PP
.BI "T&   operator[]( int " i " );"
.BI "T&   at( int " i " );"
.B  "T&   front();"
.B  "T&   back();"
.B  "int  size();"
.B  "int  max_size();"
.B  "bool empty();"
.B  "T*   data();"
.B  "T*   begin();"
.B  "T*   end();"
.BI "void fill( const T& " value " );"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
v32c++ does not implement templates. It offers
.B std::array
(and
.BR std::vector ,
see
.BR v32c++\-vector (3v32))
as built-in generic types instead: for every distinct
.BI std::array< T ", " N >
a program names, the transpiler writes one ordinary class with the
element type and length filled in, named
.BI array_ T _ N
in the generated C
.RB ( std::array<Enemy,\ 8>
becomes
.BR array_Enemy_8 ;
.B ::
and
.B *
are spelled
.B _
and
.BR _ptr ).
.B #include <array>
switches the feature on; no file is read and the line does not reach
the generated C.
.PP
.I T
may be
.BR int ,
.BR float ,
.BR bool ,
.BR char ,
any struct, class, union or enum (qualified or not), a pointer to any
of those, or another
.BR std::array .
.I N
is an integer literal, a
.B #define
constant, or a named constant such as an enum constant.
.PP
Elements of a class type are constructed when the array is, and
destroyed with it.
.SS Members
.TP
.BR operator[] ", " at ", " front ", " back
References to elements.
.B at
does not check its index: there are no exceptions.
.TP
.BR size ", " max_size ", " empty
.IR N ,
.I N
and false.
.TP
.BR data ", " begin ", " end
Plain
.B T*
pointers;
.B end
points one past the last element.
.TP
.B fill
Set every element to
.IR value .
.PP
Aggregate initialization
.RB ( "= { 1, 2, 3 }" )
works for local and global arrays, and
.B "b = a"
copies the whole array. Range-based
.B for
works over an array
.RB ( "for( Enemy &e : enemies )" ),
and so does
.B "using namespace std;"
with the bare spelling
.BR array<T,\ N> .
.PP
Accessors are written out in place in the generated C
.RB ( v[i]
becomes
.BR v.m_data[i] ,
.B v.size()
becomes the constant) unless
.B \-\-no\-inline\-containers
is given to
.BR v32c++ (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
#include <array>

struct Bunker { int x; int health; };

std::array< Bunker, 4 > bunkers;
std::array< int, 3 > speeds = { 1, 2, 4 };

void main()
{
    for( int i = 0; i < bunkers.size(); i++ )
    {
        bunkers[ i ].x = 80 + 160 * i;
        bunkers[ i ].health = 10;
    }
    for( Bunker &b : bunkers )
      b.health \-= speeds.back();
}
.fi
.RE
.SH BUGS
Lengths are compared by name:
.B std::array<int,\ MAX>
and
.B std::array<int,\ 8>
are different types even when
.B MAX
is an enum constant equal to 8 (a
.B #define
is expanded first, so it is not affected).
.PP
There is no
.B ::iterator
type name (iterators are
.BR T* ;
.B "auto it = a.begin();"
works), and range-based
.B for
does not work over a plain C array.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32c++\-vector (3v32),
.BR v32c++\-string (3v32),
.BR v32c++ (1)
.PP
.I docs/GENERICS.md
in the v32c++ source distribution.
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas
